const KEY='recall-structures-v1',STAMP='2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z';
const presets=[{"id":"arrays","title":"Arrays","notes":""},{"id":"strings","title":"Strings","notes":""},{"id":"hash-maps","title":"Hash Maps & Sets","notes":""},{"id":"linked-lists","title":"Linked Lists","notes":""},{"id":"stacks","title":"Stacks","notes":""},{"id":"sorting","title":"Sorting","notes":""},{"id":"queues","title":"Queues","notes":""},{"id":"trees","title":"Trees & BSTs","notes":""},{"id":"heaps","title":"Heaps & Priority Queues","notes":""},{"id":"graphs","title":"Graphs","notes":""},{"id":"tries","title":"Tries","notes":""},{"id":"union-find","title":"Union Find","notes":""}];
const LEGACY_DEFAULTS={"strings":"1. REVERSE A STRING\n\nBuilt-in reversal: Python's [::-1]\nExample: reversed_text = text[::-1]\n\nTwo-pointer approach:\nConvert the string into a mutable sequence of characters, then swap characters from both ends moving toward the center. Use two pointers: left starts at the first character and right at the last. Swap the characters at left and right, move both pointers inward, and repeat until they meet or cross. Finally, rebuild the string from the character sequence.\n\nPython example:\nchars = list(text)\nleft, right = 0, len(chars) - 1\nwhile left < right:\n    chars[left], chars[right] = chars[right], chars[left]\n    left += 1\n    right -= 1\nresult = ''.join(chars)\n\nTime complexity: O(n), where n is the length of the string. We must visit every character to reverse the string.\nSpace: O(n) for a new Python string / mutable character list.\n\n2. PALINDROME\n\nThe key insight is that a palindrome reads the same forwards and backwards. We can use string reversal:\n• Reverse the string.\n• Compare the reversed string with the original.\n• If they're equal, it's a palindrome.\n\nAlternative: two pointers — one at the start and one at the end. Move them toward each other while comparing characters. If any pair differs, it isn't a palindrome.\n\nTime: O(n).\nSpace: O(n) for reversal, or O(1) auxiliary for a two-pointer comparison.\n\n3. ANAGRAM\n\nTwo strings are anagrams if they contain the same characters with the same frequencies, just rearranged.\n• First check if the strings have different lengths — if so, they can't be anagrams.\n• Count the frequency of each character in the first string.\n• For each character in the second string, decrement its count.\n• If we encounter a character that doesn't exist in our count or goes below zero, they're not anagrams.\n\nTime complexity: O(n) for strings of equal length (using average O(1) hash operations).\nSpace: O(k), where k is the number of distinct characters stored.\n\n4. STRING COMPRESSION\n\nExamples:\n• \"aaabbc\" → \"a3b2c1\"\n• \"aabbccdd\" → \"a2b2c2d2\"\n• \"abc\" → \"a1b1c1\"\n\nThis demonstrates the consecutive-counting pattern, a common string-processing technique. Track when characters change as you iterate through the string:\n• Handle the edge case of an empty string.\n• Initialize a counter to 1 (for the first character).\n• Iterate through the string starting from the second character.\n• If the current character equals the previous one, increment the counter.\n• If it's different, add the previous character and its count to the result, then reset the counter.\n• Don't forget to add the last group after the loop.\n\nTime: O(n), assuming efficient output joining.\nSpace: O(n) for the new compressed string.\n\nSTRING REMINDER\nPython strings are immutable, so convert them to a list before making in-place-style changes.","linked-lists":"You have a playlist with 100 songs. You want to add a new song at position 10. With an array, you must shift 90 songs one position to the right to make room. That's 90 copy operations just to insert one item. Arrays store elements in contiguous memory, so inserting in the middle requires moving everything after it.\n\nLinked lists solve this problem by storing elements differently. Instead of keeping everything in one continuous block, linked lists scatter elements throughout memory and connect them with pointers.\n\nA linked list stores elements in nodes. Each node contains two parts: the data itself and a pointer to the next node.\n\nPYTHON EXAMPLE\nclass Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.next = None\n\n# Creating three nodes\nhead = Node(1)\nsecond = Node(2)\nthird = Node(3)\n\n# Linking them together\nhead.next = second\nsecond.next = third\n# third.next is None (null) by default\n\n# This creates: 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> None\n\nYou can access any node in a linked list by traversing from the first node to that node, but in arrays you can use an index.\n\nWHEN TO CHOOSE ARRAYS\n• You need to access elements by index frequently.\n• You're accessing elements sequentially (cache helps).\n• Memory overhead matters (linked-list nodes require additional pointers and allocation overhead).\n• You need binary search (requires sorting and random access).\n• The size is fixed or grows predictably at the end.\n\nWHEN TO CHOOSE LINKED LISTS\n• You frequently insert or delete at the beginning.\n• Size varies dramatically and unpredictably.\n• You can't allocate contiguous memory for large arrays.\n• You maintain pointers to nodes for quick modification.\n• You're building more complex structures (trees and graphs can use node-based designs).\n\nCOMPLEXITY REMINDER\n• Indexing / finding a position: O(n).\n• Add/remove at head: O(1).\n• Insert/delete after a known node: O(1).\n• Insertion at position 10 is still O(n) if you first need to traverse to find that position.","queues":"A queue is different from a regular list where you can access any position. With a queue, you only interact with the front and back. This restriction isn't a limitation — it's the point. The constraint ensures FIFO order.\n\nA queue defines what operations you can do — enqueue at the back, dequeue from the front. This makes it an abstract data type. You can implement these operations using different underlying structures like arrays or linked lists. Both maintain FIFO order, but differ in how they handle adding and removing items.\n\nTHE QUEUE CONTRACT\n• Items come out in the same order they went in.\n• You can only add to the back.\n• You can only remove from the front.\n• You can look at the front without removing it.\n\nYou can't access items in the middle. You can't remove from the back. If you want the third item, you must remove the first two items to reach it.\n\nPython tip: collections.deque supports O(1) enqueue (append), dequeue (popleft), and peek (deque[0]). Removing the first item of a Python list using pop(0) is O(n)."};
const guides={"arrays":"MENTAL MODEL\nPython list is a dynamic array: elements are stored in order and you get fast random access by index. Arrays are usually the first choice when the problem is about positions, contiguous ranges, scanning, two pointers, windows, prefix information, or sorting.\n\nInterview tip: Before coding, ask whether the problem needs random access, a contiguous subarray, repeated range queries, or an in-place transformation. Those clues often point to an array pattern.\n\nPYTHON OPERATIONS\n• Read or update by index — nums[i], nums[i] = x — O(1)\n• Append to end — nums.append(x) — O(1) amortized\n• Remove from end — nums.pop() — O(1)\n• Insert/delete near the front or middle — nums.insert(i, x), nums.pop(i), del nums[i] — O(n)\n• Search by value — x in nums, nums.index(x) — O(n)\n• Length — len(nums) — O(1)\n• Slice — nums[a:b] — O(k) time and O(k) space for k copied elements\n• Copy — nums.copy(), nums[:] — O(n)\n• Reverse in place — nums.reverse() — O(n), O(1) auxiliary space\n• Sort in place — nums.sort() — O(n log n) worst case\n• Return sorted copy — sorted(nums) — O(n log n) time and O(n) output space\n• Minimum / maximum / sum — min(nums), max(nums), sum(nums) — O(n)\n\nCORE INTERVIEW CLUES\n• \"Pair\", \"from both ends\", \"sorted array\" → two pointers\n• \"Longest/shortest contiguous subarray\" → sliding window\n• \"Range sum\", \"sum between i and j\", \"subarray sum\" → prefix sum\n• \"Find quickly\", \"seen before\", \"complement\" → hash map or set\n• \"Sorted + O(log n)\" → binary search\n• \"Next greater/smaller\" → monotonic stack\n• \"Maximum/minimum in every window\" → monotonic deque\n• \"Top K / Kth largest\" → heap, quickselect, or sorting depending on constraints\n• \"Intervals\" → usually sort first, then scan/merge\n\nTWO POINTERS TEMPLATE\nleft, right = 0, len(nums) - 1\nwhile left < right:\n    # inspect nums[left], nums[right]\n    if need_larger:\n        left += 1\n    else:\n        right -= 1\n\nTime: Usually O(n) because each pointer moves in one direction.\nSpace: Usually O(1) auxiliary.\n\nSLIDING WINDOW TEMPLATE\nleft = 0\nfor right, value in enumerate(nums):\n    # add value to window state\n    while window_is_invalid:\n        # remove nums[left] from state\n        left += 1\n    # update answer using window [left, right]\n\nKey insight: A variable window is powerful only when moving left forward can restore a useful invariant. If shrinking does not behave monotonically, sliding window may not apply.\n\nBINARY SEARCH TEMPLATE\nleft, right = 0, len(nums) - 1\n\nwhile left <= right:\n    mid = left + (right - left) // 2\n    if nums[mid] == target:\n        return mid\n    if nums[mid] < target:\n        left = mid + 1\n    else:\n        right = mid - 1\n\nreturn -1\n\nTime: O(log n).\nSpace: O(1).\n\nKey insight: Binary search requires a monotonic search space, not necessarily a literal sorted array. \"Minimum feasible X\" / \"maximum possible X\" problems often use binary search on the answer.\n\nPREFIX SUM TEMPLATE\nprefix = [0]\nfor x in nums:\n    prefix.append(prefix[-1] + x)\n\n# sum of nums[left:right+1]\nrange_sum = prefix[right + 1] - prefix[left]\n\nTime: O(n) preprocessing, then O(1) per range-sum query.\nSpace: O(n).\n\nMATRICES / 2D ARRAYS\nTreat a grid as rows × columns, and be explicit about bounds.\n\nrows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\ndirs = [(1,0), (-1,0), (0,1), (0,-1)]\n\nfor r in range(rows):\n    for c in range(cols):\n        value = grid[r][c]\n\nCommon patterns:\n• grid traversal → nested loops, O(rows × cols)\n• connected regions/islands → DFS or BFS\n• repeated rectangle sums → 2D prefix sums\n• matrix rotation / transpose → index mapping and swaps\n• sorted matrix → exploit row/column ordering if the problem guarantees it\n\nWatch: Never assume the matrix is non-empty unless constraints guarantee it.\n\nIN-PLACE THINKING\nWhen the interviewer asks for O(1) extra space, try overwriting positions you no longer need, using two pointers, swapping, or reusing the input array. Common examples: Remove Element, Move Zeroes, Sort Colors, reverse operations, and matrix transforms.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• nums[1:5] creates a copy; it is not O(1).\n• x in nums is O(n); use a set when repeated membership checks dominate.\n• nums.pop(0) and nums.insert(0, x) are O(n). Use collections.deque for queue behavior.\n• nums2 = nums does not copy; both names point to the same list.\n• Modifying a list while iterating over it can skip or mis-handle elements.\n• nums.sort() mutates the list and returns None; sorted(nums) returns a new list.\n• A nested loop is not automatically O(n²). If two pointers only move forward overall, the total may still be O(n).\n\n30-SECOND RECAP\n• Index O(1); middle insert/delete O(n); append/pop-end O(1) amortized.\n• Contiguous optimization → sliding window or prefix sum.\n• Sorted/monotonic search space → binary search O(log n).\n• Sorted pair/search problem → two pointers or binary search.\n• Grid → nested traversal, DFS/BFS, or 2D prefix sum depending on the question.\n• Repeated membership/counting → hash map/set.\n• In-place request → overwrite, swap, or pointer techniques.\n• Always state whether a slice/copy adds O(k) or O(n) space.","strings":"MENTAL MODEL\nA string is an ordered sequence of characters. In Python, strings are immutable: every operation that appears to \"change\" a string creates a new string. Most FAANG string problems are really array problems plus character rules.\n\nInterview tip: First ask whether the problem is about equality/frequency, a contiguous substring, two-ended comparison, parsing, or prefixes. That usually reveals the pattern.\n\nPYTHON OPERATIONS\n• Index character — s[i] — O(1)\n• Length — len(s) — O(1)\n• Slice — s[a:b] — O(k) time and O(k) space\n• Reverse copy — s[::-1] — O(n) time and O(n) space\n• Concatenate once — a + b — O(len(a) + len(b))\n• Repeated concatenation in a loop — can become O(n²); collect pieces then ''.join(parts)\n• Join pieces — ''.join(parts) — O(total characters)\n• Split — s.split(delim) — O(n) plus output space\n• Strip whitespace — s.strip() — O(n)\n• Character checks — ch.isalnum(), ch.isalpha(), ch.isdigit(), ch.lower() — O(1) per character for interview analysis\n• Count all frequencies — collections.Counter(s) — O(n)\n\nPALINDROME: TWO POINTERS\nleft, right = 0, len(s) - 1\nwhile left < right:\n    if s[left] != s[right]:\n        return False\n    left += 1\n    right -= 1\nreturn True\n\nTime: O(n).\nSpace: O(1) auxiliary when comparing in place.\n\nANAGRAM / FREQUENCY PATTERN\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nreturn Counter(s) == Counter(t)\n\nTime: O(n + m).\nSpace: O(k), where k is the number of distinct characters.\n\nInterview tip: For a fixed alphabet such as lowercase English letters, a 26-element integer array can replace a hash map and gives predictable O(1) alphabet space.\n\nSLIDING WINDOW FOR SUBSTRINGS\nUse sliding window when the answer must be contiguous and the window can be repaired by moving the left boundary.\n\nExample clues:\n• longest substring without repeats\n• smallest substring containing required characters\n• at most K distinct characters\n• permutation/anagram inside a larger string\n\nTypical state: a frequency map plus a left pointer.\n\nSTRING BUILDING\nparts = []\nfor item in items:\n    parts.append(transform(item))\nresult = ''.join(parts)\n\nKey insight: Building with a list and join avoids repeatedly copying an ever-growing immutable string.\n\nPARSING / ENCODING\nFor parsing, explicitly track:\n• current index\n• token boundaries\n• sign / number state\n• delimiters\n• malformed or edge input\n\nFor encode/decode problems, length-prefix encoding is robust because the payload may itself contain delimiter characters.\n\nExample: \"hello\" → \"5#hello\". Read digits until '#', then read exactly that many characters.\n\nPREFIX PROBLEMS\n• Common prefix across strings → scan character columns or progressively shrink a candidate prefix.\n• Many prefix searches / autocomplete → Trie.\n• Exact membership only → set is usually simpler than a Trie.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Strings are immutable; s[i] = 'x' is invalid.\n• s[::-1] is concise but allocates O(n) extra space.\n• Repeated s += piece may be quadratic in interview reasoning; prefer join.\n• Normalize only when the problem allows it. Lowercasing, removing punctuation, or sorting changes semantics.\n• Be explicit about empty string, one-character string, repeated characters, spaces, and case sensitivity.\n• Sorting two strings to test anagrams costs O(n log n); frequency counting can be O(n).\n\n30-SECOND RECAP\n• Python strings are immutable.\n• Palindrome → two pointers.\n• Anagram/frequency → Counter or hash map.\n• Contiguous substring optimization → sliding window.\n• Build output → list + join.\n• Prefix-heavy dictionary operations → Trie.\n• Always account for slicing/reversal copies in space complexity.","hash-maps":"MENTAL MODEL\nA hash map stores key → value pairs; a set stores unique keys only. They trade extra memory for very fast average lookup. In Python: dict for maps, set for sets.\n\nInterview tip: If you hear \"find quickly\", \"seen before\", \"frequency\", \"group by\", \"complement\", \"deduplicate\", or \"count subarrays\", think hashing.\n\nPYTHON DICT OPERATIONS\n• Read existing key — d[key] — O(1) average\n• Safe lookup — d.get(key, default) — O(1) average\n• Insert / update — d[key] = value — O(1) average\n• Membership — key in d — O(1) average\n• Delete — del d[key], d.pop(key, default) — O(1) average\n• Iterate — for key, value in d.items() — O(n)\n• Number of keys — len(d) — O(1)\n• Initialize list buckets — defaultdict(list)\n• Count frequencies — Counter(iterable)\n• Increment safely — d[x] = d.get(x, 0) + 1\n\nPYTHON SET OPERATIONS\n• Add — seen.add(x) — O(1) average\n• Membership — x in seen — O(1) average\n• Remove if present — seen.discard(x) — O(1) average\n• Remove and error if absent — seen.remove(x) — O(1) average\n• Union — a | b — roughly O(len(a) + len(b))\n• Intersection — a & b — roughly O(min(len(a), len(b))) average\n• Difference — a - b — O(len(a)) average\n\nComplexity: Hash operations are O(1) average, but O(n) worst case in pathological collision scenarios. For interviews, state \"average O(1)\" unless the interviewer asks about worst-case hashing.\n\nCORE PATTERN: TWO SUM / COMPLEMENT LOOKUP\nseen = {}\nfor i, x in enumerate(nums):\n    need = target - x\n    if need in seen:\n        return [seen[need], i]\n    seen[x] = i\n\nTime: O(n) average.\nSpace: O(n).\n\nCORE PATTERN: FREQUENCY COUNTING\nfrom collections import Counter\nfreq = Counter(nums)\n\nUse for duplicates, anagrams, majority/frequency tasks, top-K frequency, and window counts.\n\nCORE PATTERN: GROUPING\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ngroups = defaultdict(list)\nfor item in items:\n    key = canonical_key(item)\n    groups[key].append(item)\n\nKey insight: The hard part is often designing a canonical, hashable key. For anagrams, that might be sorted characters or a tuple of 26 counts.\n\nCORE PATTERN: PREFIX SUM + HASH MAP\ncount = 0\nprefix = 0\nseen = {0: 1}\n\nfor x in nums:\n    prefix += x\n    count += seen.get(prefix - k, 0)\n    seen[prefix] = seen.get(prefix, 0) + 1\n\nTime: O(n) average.\nSpace: O(n).\n\nWhy {0: 1}: It represents an empty prefix so a subarray starting at index 0 can be counted.\n\nSET PATTERN: SEQUENCE STARTS\nFor Longest Consecutive Sequence:\n• Put all values in a set.\n• Start a sequence only when x - 1 is not in the set.\n• Walk forward x + 1, x + 2, ...\nEach number belongs to at most one forward walk, so total work is O(n) average.\n\nHASHABLE KEYS\nValid dict/set keys must be hashable: int, str, tuple of hashable values, frozenset.\nLists, dicts, and normal sets are mutable and cannot be keys.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• d[key] raises KeyError when missing; use get/defaultdict when absence is normal.\n• set.remove(x) raises KeyError if x is absent; discard(x) does not.\n• A dict preserves insertion order in modern Python, but that does not mean keys are sorted.\n• Do not mutate a dict/set while iterating over it unless you iterate over a safe copy.\n• Counter equality/count behavior can include zero or negative counts after manual updates; understand what your algorithm expects.\n• Hashing improves time by spending memory. Be ready to explain the O(n) space trade-off.\n• Never use a list as a dict key; convert a fixed structure to tuple.\n\n30-SECOND RECAP\n• dict/set lookup, insert, delete: O(1) average.\n• Duplicates / seen-before → set.\n• Frequency / mapping / complement → dict or Counter.\n• Grouping → defaultdict(list) + canonical key.\n• Subarray sum = K → prefix sum + frequency map.\n• Keys must be hashable.\n• Always say average O(1), O(n) worst-case hashing if asked.","linked-lists":"MENTAL MODEL\nA linked list stores values in nodes connected by references instead of contiguous memory. You cannot jump to index i; you must follow links from a known node.\n\nKey insight: Linked-list interview problems are mostly about pointer ownership. Before changing node.next, decide whether you still need the old next node and save it first.\n\nNODE TEMPLATE\nclass ListNode:\n    def __init__(self, val=0, next=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.next = next\n\nCOMPLEXITY\n• Access kth/indexed element — O(n)\n• Search by value — O(n)\n• Insert/remove at head — O(1)\n• Insert after a known node — O(1)\n• Delete after a known predecessor — O(1)\n• Append with a stored tail pointer — O(1); otherwise O(n) to find the tail\n• Reverse entire list — O(n) time, O(1) auxiliary space iteratively\n\nInterview tip: \"O(1) insertion\" only applies when the relevant node/reference is already known. Finding position 10 still costs O(n).\n\nCORE PATTERN: REVERSE A LIST\nprev = None\ncurr = head\n\nwhile curr:\n    nxt = curr.next\n    curr.next = prev\n    prev = curr\n    curr = nxt\n\nreturn prev\n\nTime: O(n).\nSpace: O(1).\n\nInvariant: prev is the already-reversed prefix; curr is the first node not yet reversed.\n\nCORE PATTERN: DUMMY / SENTINEL NODE\ndummy = ListNode(0, head)\nprev = dummy\n\nUse a dummy node when the head itself may be inserted, removed, or replaced. It removes special-case logic for index 0.\n\nCommon problems:\n• remove nth from end\n• merge lists\n• partition list\n• delete duplicates\n• add two numbers\n\nCORE PATTERN: FAST / SLOW POINTERS\nslow = fast = head\nwhile fast and fast.next:\n    slow = slow.next\n    fast = fast.next.next\n\nUses:\n• find middle\n• detect cycle\n• split list in half\n• palindrome/reorder list\n\nNTH FROM END / FIXED GAP\nUse two pointers separated by n nodes. A dummy node makes deleting the head safe.\n\ndummy = ListNode(0, head)\nslow = fast = dummy\n\nfor _ in range(n + 1):\n    fast = fast.next\n\nwhile fast:\n    slow = slow.next\n    fast = fast.next\n\nslow.next = slow.next.next\nreturn dummy.next\n\nTime: O(length).\nSpace: O(1).\n\nKey insight: The fixed gap converts \"count from the end\" into one forward pass.\n\nCYCLE DETECTION\nIf slow and fast meet, a cycle exists. Floyd's algorithm uses O(1) extra space.\n\nTo find the cycle entry after a meeting:\n• Move one pointer back to head.\n• Move both one step at a time.\n• Their next meeting is the cycle entry.\n\nMERGE TWO SORTED LISTS\ndummy = tail = ListNode()\n\nwhile a and b:\n    if a.val <= b.val:\n        tail.next, a = a, a.next\n    else:\n        tail.next, b = b, b.next\n    tail = tail.next\n\ntail.next = a or b\nreturn dummy.next\n\nTime: O(n + m).\nSpace: O(1) auxiliary when reusing nodes.\n\nREORDER LIST THINKING\nA common compound interview pattern:\n1. Find middle with fast/slow.\n2. Reverse the second half.\n3. Merge the two halves alternately.\n\nDOUBLY LINKED LISTS\nEach node has prev and next. Given a node, removal can be O(1) because both neighbors are directly available.\nThis is why LRU Cache commonly combines:\n• hash map: key → node, O(1) average lookup\n• doubly linked list: O(1) remove/move-to-front\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Overwriting curr.next before saving it loses the rest of the list.\n• Returning dummy instead of dummy.next.\n• Forgetting fast and fast.next checks.\n• Creating accidental cycles while rewiring.\n• Comparing nodes instead of node.val when the problem asks about values.\n• Assuming linked lists are automatically faster than arrays; they have poorer cache locality and no O(1) indexing.\n• Recursive reversal uses O(n) call-stack space even though the pointer work itself is O(n).\n\n30-SECOND RECAP\n• No random access: indexing/search O(n).\n• Known-node insert/delete can be O(1).\n• Reverse → prev/curr/nxt.\n• Head may change → dummy node.\n• Middle/cycle → fast & slow.\n• LRU → hash map + doubly linked list.\n• Save next before rewiring.","stacks":"MENTAL MODEL\nA stack is LIFO: last in, first out. It is ideal when the newest unresolved item must be handled first. In Python, a list is usually the best stack.\n\nPYTHON OPERATIONS\n• Push — stack.append(x) — O(1) amortized\n• Pop top — stack.pop() — O(1)\n• Peek top — stack[-1] — O(1)\n• Empty check — if not stack — O(1)\n• Size — len(stack) — O(1)\n\nInterview tip: \"nested\", \"matching\", \"undo\", \"evaluate expression\", \"next greater/smaller\", \"previous greater/smaller\", or \"process in reverse order\" are strong stack clues.\n\nBALANCED PARENTHESES TEMPLATE\npairs = {')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{'}\nstack = []\n\nfor ch in s:\n    if ch in pairs.values():\n        stack.append(ch)\n    elif ch in pairs:\n        if not stack or stack.pop() != pairs[ch]:\n            return False\n\nreturn not stack\n\nTime: O(n).\nSpace: O(n) worst case.\n\nEXPRESSION / RPN PATTERN\nFor Reverse Polish Notation:\n• Number → push.\n• Operator → pop right operand, then left operand.\n• Compute and push result.\n\nWatch: Operand order matters for subtraction and division.\n\nMONOTONIC STACK\nA monotonic stack keeps values or indices in increasing or decreasing order. When a new value violates the order, pop until the invariant is restored.\n\nExample: next greater element / Daily Temperatures.\n\nstack = []  # indices\nfor i, x in enumerate(nums):\n    while stack and nums[stack[-1]] < x:\n        j = stack.pop()\n        answer[j] = i - j\n    stack.append(i)\n\nTime: O(n), not O(n²). Each index is pushed once and popped at most once.\nSpace: O(n).\n\nWHEN TO STORE INDICES\nStore indices instead of values when you need:\n• distance between positions\n• write an answer back to the original position\n• distinguish duplicate values\n\nMIN STACK IDEA\nStore either:\n• pairs (value, minimum_so_far), or\n• a second stack of minima.\n\nThen push, pop, top, and getMin can all be O(1).\n\nSTACK AS ITERATIVE DFS\nstack = [start]\nwhile stack:\n    node = stack.pop()\n    # process node\n    for nei in neighbors(node):\n        stack.append(nei)\n\nWatch: Use a visited set for graphs with cycles.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• list.pop(0) is not stack behavior and is O(n).\n• Calling stack.pop() without checking emptiness can fail.\n• In RPN, first pop is the right operand, second pop is the left operand.\n• Monotonic stack direction depends on the question; define the invariant in words before coding.\n• A while loop inside a for loop can still be O(n) when each element is popped only once.\n• Recursive DFS implicitly uses the call stack; iterative DFS makes that stack explicit.\n\n30-SECOND RECAP\n• Python stack = list.\n• append/pop/top are O(1) amortized/O(1).\n• Parentheses/parsing → normal stack.\n• Next greater/smaller → monotonic stack.\n• Need distance → store indices.\n• Explain amortized O(n): every item pushed/popped at most once.","sorting":"MENTAL MODEL\nSorting converts an unordered problem into an ordered one, often making relationships local and easier to scan. The trade-off is usually O(n log n) time.\n\nInterview tip: If ordering lets you use two pointers, merge intervals, greedily choose items, detect duplicates, or compare neighbors, sorting is often worth the cost.\n\nPYTHON SORTING\nnums.sort()\n# Mutates nums and returns None.\n\nordered = sorted(nums)\n# Returns a new list; original is unchanged.\n\npeople.sort(key=lambda p: (p.age, p.name))\nnums.sort(reverse=True)\n\nTime: Python's Timsort is O(n log n) worst case and can approach O(n) on already-partially-ordered runs.\nSpace: sorted(...) needs O(n) for the returned list; Python sorting may also use auxiliary memory internally.\n\nSTABILITY\nPython sorting is stable: items with equal keys preserve their original relative order.\n\nWhy it matters: You can sort by a secondary key first, then by a primary key, and equal primary keys retain the secondary ordering.\n\nTUPLE / CUSTOM KEYS\nPairs/tuples sort lexicographically by default:\n(1, 5) < (2, 0)\n(1, 3) < (1, 8)\n\nUse key= to express exactly what ordering the algorithm needs.\n\nCORE PATTERN: SORT + TWO POINTERS\nnums.sort()\nleft, right = 0, len(nums) - 1\n\nwhile left < right:\n    total = nums[left] + nums[right]\n    if total == target:\n        ...\n    elif total < target:\n        left += 1\n    else:\n        right -= 1\n\nTime: O(n log n) because sorting dominates.\nSpace: Depends on sorting/output requirements.\n\nCORE PATTERN: INTERVALS\nintervals.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\nmerged = []\n\nfor start, end in intervals:\n    if not merged or start > merged[-1][1]:\n        merged.append([start, end])\n    else:\n        merged[-1][1] = max(merged[-1][1], end)\n\nTime: O(n log n).\nSpace: O(n) for output in the worst case.\n\nCOMMON ALGORITHMS TO KNOW\nMERGE SORT\n• Divide array in half, sort halves, merge.\n• O(n log n) worst-case time.\n• O(n) auxiliary space in the usual array implementation.\n• Stable.\n• Excellent when guaranteed O(n log n) matters.\n\nQUICKSORT\n• Partition around a pivot, recurse on partitions.\n• O(n log n) average.\n• O(n²) worst case with poor pivots/inputs.\n• Often O(log n) average recursion stack and in-place-style partitioning.\n• Usually not stable.\n\nHEAP SORT\n• Build heap, repeatedly remove extreme.\n• O(n log n) worst-case.\n• Can be O(1) auxiliary with an in-place array heap implementation.\n• Not stable.\n\nCOUNTING SORT\n• Count each value, then rebuild.\n• O(n + k) time and O(k) extra space, where k is the value range.\n• Useful only when keys are integers in a reasonably small known range.\n\nBUCKET / RADIX IDEA\nUse structure in the key domain to beat comparison sorting. These are constraint-dependent; always state assumptions.\n\nDUTCH NATIONAL FLAG\nFor exactly three categories such as 0/1/2, maintain low, current, high partitions to sort in O(n) time and O(1) space.\n\nWHEN NOT TO FULLY SORT\n• Need only K smallest/largest → heap O(n log k) or quickselect average O(n).\n• Already sorted and searching → binary search O(log n).\n• Small integer domain → counting/bucket techniques.\n• Need only min/max → one O(n) scan.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• nums.sort() returns None.\n• Sorting changes indices; if original positions matter, store (value, index) pairs.\n• Sorting an array just to use a set-like lookup may worsen O(n) to O(n log n).\n• Custom comparator logic is usually better expressed as key= in Python.\n• Be explicit about whether mutating the input is allowed.\n• After sorting, handling duplicates often requires skipping equal neighbors deliberately.\n\n30-SECOND RECAP\n• Python sort: O(n log n) worst case, stable.\n• sort() mutates; sorted() copies.\n• Sorting unlocks two pointers, interval merging, greedy scans.\n• Merge sort: guaranteed O(n log n), O(n) space.\n• Quicksort: average O(n log n), worst O(n²).\n• Need only top K? Consider heap/quickselect instead of full sort.","queues":"MENTAL MODEL\nA queue is FIFO: first in, first out. Use it when work must be processed in arrival order or by distance/layer. In Python, use collections.deque.\n\nPYTHON DEQUE OPERATIONS\nfrom collections import deque\n\nq = deque()\nq.append(x)       # enqueue right\nx = q.popleft()   # dequeue left\nfront = q[0]      # peek left\nq.appendleft(x)   # add left\nx = q.pop()       # remove right\n\nComplexity: append, appendleft, pop, and popleft are O(1). Indexing near the middle of a deque is O(n), so do not treat it like a random-access array.\n\nInterview tip: \"minimum number of steps\" in an unweighted graph, \"level order\", \"nearest\", \"spread simultaneously\", or \"process in arrival order\" strongly suggests BFS + queue.\n\nBFS TEMPLATE\nfrom collections import deque\n\nq = deque([start])\nseen = {start}\n\nwhile q:\n    node = q.popleft()\n    for nei in neighbors(node):\n        if nei not in seen:\n            seen.add(nei)\n            q.append(nei)\n\nTime: O(V + E) for an adjacency-list graph.\nSpace: O(V).\n\nKey insight: Mark visited when ENQUEUING, not later when dequeuing. This prevents the same node from entering the queue multiple times.\n\nLEVEL-ORDER TEMPLATE\nq = deque([root])\n\nwhile q:\n    level_size = len(q)\n    level = []\n\n    for _ in range(level_size):\n        node = q.popleft()\n        level.append(node.val)\n\n        if node.left:\n            q.append(node.left)\n        if node.right:\n            q.append(node.right)\n\n    # level contains one tree depth\n\nMULTI-SOURCE BFS\nInitialize the queue with ALL starting sources at distance 0.\n\nUse for:\n• Rotting Oranges\n• distance to nearest gate/zero\n• simultaneous infection/spread\n• closest source for every cell\n\nThis avoids running a separate BFS from every source.\n\nSHORTEST PATH RULE\nBFS gives shortest path by number of edges only when every edge has equal cost (or the graph is unweighted).\n\nFor nonnegative weighted edges, think Dijkstra + min heap.\n\nDEQUE AS A MONOTONIC QUEUE\nA deque can maintain candidates for a sliding-window maximum/minimum.\nEach index is added and removed at most once → O(n) total.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Python list pop(0) is O(n). Use deque.popleft().\n• Marking visited too late can create many duplicate queue entries.\n• BFS is not automatically correct for weighted shortest paths.\n• For level problems, capture level_size before processing that level.\n• A deque supports both ends efficiently but not fast arbitrary middle indexing.\n\n30-SECOND RECAP\n• Queue = FIFO; Python = deque.\n• append/popleft O(1).\n• Unweighted shortest path → BFS.\n• Tree levels → process len(q) nodes per level.\n• Many simultaneous starts → multi-source BFS.\n• Sliding-window max/min → monotonic deque.\n• Mark visited when enqueuing.","trees":"MENTAL MODEL\nA tree is a connected acyclic hierarchy. Most tree interview solutions are DFS or BFS plus a carefully chosen meaning for what each recursive call returns.\n\nFor a binary tree:\nclass TreeNode:\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.left = left\n        self.right = right\n\nCOMPLEXITY LANGUAGE\nLet n = number of nodes and h = tree height.\n• Full traversal — O(n)\n• Recursive DFS stack — O(h)\n• Balanced tree h ≈ O(log n)\n• Completely skewed tree h = O(n)\n• BFS queue — O(width), up to O(n)\n\nDFS ORDERS\nPREORDER: node → left → right\nUseful for copying/serializing structure and top-down decisions.\n\nINORDER: left → node → right\nFor a BST, inorder yields values in sorted order.\n\nPOSTORDER: left → right → node\nUseful when a parent answer depends on completed child answers: height, balance, diameter, subtree sums.\n\nRECURSIVE DFS TEMPLATE\ndef dfs(node):\n    if not node:\n        return base_value\n\n    left = dfs(node.left)\n    right = dfs(node.right)\n\n    return combine(node, left, right)\n\nInterview tip: Before coding recursion, say one sentence: \"dfs(node) returns ____.\" That sentence often determines the whole solution.\n\nBFS / LEVEL ORDER\nfrom collections import deque\n\nq = deque([root])\nwhile q:\n    for _ in range(len(q)):\n        node = q.popleft()\n        if node.left:\n            q.append(node.left)\n        if node.right:\n            q.append(node.right)\n\nUse for level order, minimum depth, right-side view, and nearest-by-level questions.\n\nBINARY SEARCH TREE INVARIANT\nFor every node:\n• all values in left subtree are smaller\n• all values in right subtree are larger\nassuming distinct keys\n\nBST operations:\n• Search/insert/delete — O(h)\n• Balanced BST — O(log n)\n• Worst-case skewed BST — O(n)\n\nVALIDATE BST WITH BOUNDS\ndef valid(node, low, high):\n    if not node:\n        return True\n    if not (low < node.val < high):\n        return False\n    return valid(node.left, low, node.val) and valid(node.right, node.val, high)\n\nKey insight: Comparing only a node with its direct children is NOT enough. Ancestor bounds matter.\n\nLOWEST COMMON ANCESTOR IN BST\nUse ordering:\n• both targets < node → go left\n• both > node → go right\n• otherwise current node is the split point / LCA\n\nBOTTOM-UP POSTORDER PATTERN\nDiameter / balanced tree / max path-style problems often need children to return information upward.\n\nExample for height:\ndef height(node):\n    if not node:\n        return 0\n    return 1 + max(height(node.left), height(node.right))\n\nFor diameter, compute left/right heights and update a global/nonlocal best with left + right.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Missing the None base case.\n• Confusing node depth with subtree height.\n• Returning the global answer instead of the information the parent needs.\n• Assuming BSTs are automatically balanced.\n• Validating BST using only immediate children.\n• Forgetting recursion can use O(n) stack space on a skewed tree.\n• In path problems, clarify whether a path must start at root, end at leaf, or can start/end anywhere.\n\n30-SECOND RECAP\n• Traversal O(n); recursion O(h).\n• Parent needs child results → postorder DFS.\n• Level/min-depth → BFS.\n• BST inorder is sorted.\n• BST operation O(h): O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed.\n• Validate BST with ancestor bounds.\n• Define exactly what dfs(node) returns before coding.","heaps":"MENTAL MODEL\nA heap is a partially ordered tree stored compactly in an array. Python heapq is a MIN-HEAP: the smallest element is always at index 0. A heap is for repeatedly getting/removing the best candidate, not for fully sorting everything.\n\nPYTHON HEAP OPERATIONS\nimport heapq\n\nheapq.heapify(nums)        # O(n)\nheapq.heappush(heap, x)    # O(log n)\nx = heapq.heappop(heap)    # O(log n)\nsmallest = heap[0]         # O(1)\nheapq.heappushpop(heap, x) # O(log n)\nheapq.heapreplace(heap, x) # O(log n)\n\nInterview tip: \"Kth largest/smallest\", \"top K\", \"always process smallest cost\", \"merge K sorted\", \"scheduling\", or \"best candidate changes over time\" suggests a heap.\n\nMAX HEAP IN PYTHON\nPython's common interview pattern:\nheapq.heappush(heap, -x)\nlargest = -heapq.heappop(heap)\n\nFor tuples, heapq compares lexicographically:\n(priority, tie_breaker, value)\n\nTOP K PATTERN\nKeep a min-heap of size k containing the k largest items.\n\nheap = []\nfor x in nums:\n    heapq.heappush(heap, x)\n    if len(heap) > k:\n        heapq.heappop(heap)\n\nanswer = heap[0]  # kth largest\n\nTime: O(n log k).\nSpace: O(k).\n\nWhy min-heap for K largest: the smallest of the current winners sits at the root and is the easiest candidate to evict.\n\nK-WAY MERGE\nPut the first item from each sorted source into a heap with source metadata. Every pop reveals the next globally smallest item; then push the next item from that same source.\n\nTime: O(N log k), where N is total items and k is number of sources.\n\nDIJKSTRA CONNECTION\nFor nonnegative weighted shortest paths, use a min-heap of (distance, node). The heap chooses the next smallest tentative distance.\n\nImportant: heapq does not support a native decrease-key operation; commonly push a new pair and skip stale entries when popped.\n\nHEAP VS SORT\nUse sorting when:\n• you need all items ordered\n• one-time ordering is enough\n\nUse heap when:\n• you repeatedly need the current min/max\n• only top K matters\n• candidates arrive incrementally\n\nComplexity comparison:\n• Full sort — O(n log n)\n• Top K with heap — O(n log k)\n• Heapify all — O(n), then each pop O(log n)\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• A heap is NOT globally sorted; only heap[0] is guaranteed minimum.\n• Iterating the heap array does not produce sorted order.\n• Arbitrary deletion/search inside a heap is O(n) unless you add extra bookkeeping.\n• For a max heap using negatives, remember to negate consistently on push/pop.\n• Tuples with equal priorities may try to compare the next fields; use an integer tie-breaker if later objects are not comparable.\n• heapify is O(n), not O(n log n).\n\n30-SECOND RECAP\n• Python heapq = min-heap.\n• Root O(1); push/pop O(log n); heapify O(n).\n• Top K → heap size k → O(n log k).\n• K sorted streams → heap of k heads.\n• Weighted shortest path → Dijkstra + min heap.\n• Heap is partial order, not a sorted list.","graphs":"MENTAL MODEL\nA graph models entities (vertices) and relationships (edges). The first interview decision is usually representation + traversal + visited state.\n\nREPRESENTATIONS\nADJACENCY LIST\ngraph = {\n    0: [1, 2],\n    1: [0],\n    2: [0]\n}\n\nSpace: O(V + E).\nBest default for sparse graphs and most interview problems.\n\nADJACENCY MATRIX\nmatrix[u][v] indicates an edge.\nSpace: O(V²).\nEdge lookup: O(1).\nUseful when graph is dense or constant-time edge checks dominate.\n\nDIRECTED VS UNDIRECTED\nUndirected edge u-v is usually stored both ways.\nDirected u→v is stored only from u to v.\n\nInterview tip: Clarify direction, weights, possible cycles, whether all nodes are listed, and whether the graph can be disconnected.\n\nDFS TEMPLATE\ndef dfs(node):\n    if node in seen:\n        return\n    seen.add(node)\n\n    for nei in graph[node]:\n        dfs(nei)\n\nTime: O(V + E).\nSpace: O(V) including visited and recursion in worst case.\n\nBFS TEMPLATE\nfrom collections import deque\n\nq = deque([start])\nseen = {start}\n\nwhile q:\n    node = q.popleft()\n    for nei in graph[node]:\n        if nei not in seen:\n            seen.add(nei)\n            q.append(nei)\n\nTime: O(V + E).\nSpace: O(V).\n\nBFS VS DFS\nUse BFS when:\n• shortest path in unweighted graph\n• minimum number of edges/steps\n• level-by-level expansion\n• multi-source distance\n\nUse DFS when:\n• explore a whole component\n• detect/reason about structure\n• backtracking-like traversal\n• topological DFS\n• subtree/recursive logic feels natural\n\nCONNECTED COMPONENTS\nLoop over every vertex. If unseen, start DFS/BFS and increment component count.\n\nThis handles disconnected graphs; traversing only from node 0 does not.\n\nTOPOLOGICAL SORT: KAHN BFS\nUsed for prerequisite/dependency ordering in a directed acyclic graph.\n\n1. Compute indegree of every node.\n2. Queue all nodes with indegree 0.\n3. Pop one, append to order, decrement neighbors.\n4. If processed count < V, a directed cycle exists.\n\nTime: O(V + E).\n\nSHORTEST PATH TOOLBOX\n• Unweighted / equal weights → BFS, O(V + E)\n• Nonnegative weights → Dijkstra + min heap, typically O((V + E) log V)\n• Negative edges → Bellman-Ford class of algorithms; Dijkstra is not valid\n• All-pairs small dense graph → Floyd-Warshall O(V³), depending on constraints\n\nGRID AS GRAPH\nEach cell is a node; edges connect allowed neighboring cells.\n\nTypical directions:\ndirs = [(1,0), (-1,0), (0,1), (0,-1)]\n\nCommon problems: islands, flood fill, shortest path in maze, rotting/spread.\n\nCYCLE DETECTION\nUndirected graph:\n• DFS with parent, or\n• Union Find\n\nDirected graph:\n• DFS with visiting/visited states, or\n• topological processed-count check\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Forgetting a visited set and looping forever on cycles.\n• Marking visited only when dequeued, causing duplicate queue entries.\n• Treating a directed graph as undirected.\n• Forgetting disconnected components.\n• Using BFS for weighted shortest path.\n• Recursive DFS may hit Python recursion limits on very deep graphs; iterative DFS can avoid that.\n• Complexity is O(V + E), not automatically O(V²), when using adjacency lists.\n\n30-SECOND RECAP\n• Default representation: adjacency list O(V + E) space.\n• DFS/BFS traversal: O(V + E).\n• Unweighted shortest path → BFS.\n• Dependencies → topological sort.\n• Nonnegative weighted shortest path → Dijkstra.\n• Components → outer loop + DFS/BFS.\n• Always clarify direction, weights, cycles, disconnected nodes.","tries":"MENTAL MODEL\nA Trie (prefix tree) stores strings character by character so shared prefixes share nodes. It is useful when PREFIX queries matter repeatedly.\n\nNODE TEMPLATE\nclass TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.children = {}\n        self.is_end = False\n\nINSERT\ndef insert(word):\n    node = root\n    for ch in word:\n        if ch not in node.children:\n            node.children[ch] = TrieNode()\n        node = node.children[ch]\n    node.is_end = True\n\nTime: O(L), where L is word length.\n\nEXACT SEARCH\ndef search(word):\n    node = root\n    for ch in word:\n        if ch not in node.children:\n            return False\n        node = node.children[ch]\n    return node.is_end\n\nTime: O(L).\n\nPREFIX SEARCH\ndef starts_with(prefix):\n    node = root\n    for ch in prefix:\n        if ch not in node.children:\n            return False\n        node = node.children[ch]\n    return True\n\nTime: O(L).\n\nWHY is_end MATTERS\nIf \"app\" and \"apple\" are inserted, the node for the second 'p' must remember that \"app\" is a complete word. Reaching a node is not enough to prove exact-word membership.\n\nWHEN TO USE A TRIE\n• autocomplete / typeahead\n• dictionary prefix queries\n• word search with many candidate words\n• longest prefix matching\n• replace words by roots\n• prefix counts\n\nWHEN NOT TO USE IT\nIf you only need exact membership, a hash set is usually simpler and gives O(1) average lookup versus O(L) traversal.\n\nWORD SEARCH II IDEA\nCombine:\n• Trie for pruning prefixes\n• DFS/backtracking on the board\n\nAs soon as the current board path is not a Trie prefix, stop exploring that branch. This can dramatically reduce useless search.\n\nSPACE\nTime: insert/search/prefix O(L).\nSpace: O(total created Trie nodes), proportional to the total number of characters not shared by prefixes.\n\nA children dict is memory-flexible. A fixed array of 26 children can be faster/predictable for lowercase English but uses more memory per node.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Forgetting is_end and treating every prefix as a full word.\n• Recreating the root inside methods accidentally.\n• Using a Trie when a set is sufficient.\n• In board-search problems, forgetting to restore a cell after backtracking.\n• Returning after finding one word when multiple words may share the same prefix.\n• Assuming Trie lookup is O(1); it is O(L).\n\n30-SECOND RECAP\n• Trie = shared-prefix tree.\n• Insert/search/prefix = O(L).\n• Exact word requires is_end.\n• Prefix-heavy workload → Trie.\n• Exact membership only → set is often simpler.\n• Board word search → Trie + DFS pruning.","union-find":"MENTAL MODEL\nUnion Find / Disjoint Set Union (DSU) maintains groups under repeated merges. It answers: \"Are these two items in the same connected component?\" very efficiently.\n\nCORE DATA\nparent[x] = representative parent of x\nsize[x] or rank[x] = heuristic for keeping trees shallow\n\nINITIALIZATION\nparent = list(range(n))\nsize = [1] * n\n\nEach item starts as its own component.\n\nFIND WITH PATH COMPRESSION\ndef find(x):\n    while x != parent[x]:\n        parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n        x = parent[x]\n    return x\n\nPath compression rewires nodes toward the root so future finds become faster.\n\nUNION BY SIZE\ndef union(a, b):\n    ra, rb = find(a), find(b)\n    if ra == rb:\n        return False\n\n    if size[ra] < size[rb]:\n        ra, rb = rb, ra\n\n    parent[rb] = ra\n    size[ra] += size[rb]\n    return True\n\nReturning False is useful for detecting an edge that connects two vertices already in the same component.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nWith path compression + union by size/rank:\n• find — amortized O(α(n))\n• union — amortized O(α(n))\n• α(n), inverse Ackermann, grows so slowly that it is effectively constant for practical input sizes\n• initialization — O(n)\n• space — O(n)\n\nInterview tip: Do not casually say \"strict O(1)\"; say \"amortized O(α(n)), effectively constant.\"\n\nWHEN TO USE DSU\n• dynamic undirected connectivity\n• count connected components while edges are added\n• redundant connection / cycle detection in undirected graph\n• merge accounts / identities\n• Kruskal minimum spanning tree\n• grouping equivalent relationships\n\nREDUNDANT EDGE PATTERN\nFor each undirected edge (u, v):\n• if union(u, v) returns False, u and v were already connected\n• therefore this edge creates a cycle / is redundant\n\nCOMPONENT COUNT\nStart components = n.\nEvery successful union reduces components by 1.\n\nKRUSKAL CONNECTION\n1. Sort weighted edges by weight.\n2. Add an edge only if its endpoints are currently in different DSU components.\n3. Successful unions build the minimum spanning tree.\n\nTime: sorting dominates at O(E log E); DSU operations are near-constant amortized.\n\nDSU VS DFS/BFS\nUse DFS/BFS when:\n• graph is mostly static\n• you need paths, distances, traversal order, or actual nodes in a route\n\nUse DSU when:\n• many union/connectivity operations happen\n• you only need component membership, not the path between nodes\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• DSU does not give shortest paths.\n• Basic DSU is for undirected connectivity; directed reachability is a different problem.\n• Forgetting path compression or union-by-size can degrade performance.\n• Comparing parent[x] directly is unsafe unless x is already a root; compare find(a) == find(b).\n• Updating size/rank on the child root instead of the new root.\n• Coordinate/string inputs may need mapping to integer IDs or a dict-based parent structure.\n\n30-SECOND RECAP\n• DSU answers repeated connectivity + merge queries.\n• find = representative; union = merge representatives.\n• Path compression + union by size/rank.\n• Amortized O(α(n)) per operation.\n• Redundant undirected edge → union returns false.\n• Component count drops on each successful union.\n• Need actual path/distance? Use graph traversal instead."};
